1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:08,337
The brush uncovers a carved panel: a tree, an empty seat beneath it, a parasol above, and a pair of footprints in front.

2
00:00:08,337 --> 00:00:12,878
Someone is clearly the subject of this carving, and he is not in it.

3
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:22,262
For the first few centuries, this is exactly how Buddhist art worked — the Buddha was shown by the space he left.

4
00:00:22,262 --> 00:00:25,008
This series keeps that convention throughout.

5
00:00:29,467 --> 00:00:34,347
From the Buddhist canon, preserved in Pali and written down centuries after his death;

6
00:00:34,347 --> 00:00:39,002
from later biographies, which add a great deal; and from archaeology at the sites.

7
00:00:39,002 --> 00:00:44,687
Historians separate what the earliest texts say from what grew around it later, and so will we.

8
00:00:49,967 --> 00:00:52,752
Here is the life as the tradition tells it.

9
00:00:52,752 --> 00:00:58,639
He was born Siddhartha Gautama, into the Shakya ganasangha at Lumbini, in what is now Nepal.

10
00:00:58,639 --> 00:01:00,095
He grew up in comfort.

11
00:01:00,095 --> 00:01:05,222
Then, the tradition says, he saw four sights — an old man, a sick man, a corpse,

12
00:01:05,222 --> 00:01:11,725
and a wandering ascetic — and understood that comfort is temporary and suffering is not optional.

13
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:18,256
He left home.

14
00:01:18,256 --> 00:01:23,394
He studied with teachers and practised severe austerity for years, nearly starving.

15
00:01:23,394 --> 00:01:30,245
He concluded that neither indulgence nor self-torture works, and found enlightenment under a tree at Bodh Gaya.

16
00:01:30,245 --> 00:01:35,811
He gave his first sermon at Sarnath, near Varanasi, and taught for about forty-five years.

17
00:01:35,811 --> 00:01:37,518
He died at Kushinagar.

18
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:46,311
The teaching begins with four propositions, the Four Noble Truths.

19
00:01:46,311 --> 00:01:48,069
One: there is suffering.

20
00:01:48,069 --> 00:01:51,303
Two: suffering has a cause, which is craving.

21
00:01:51,303 --> 00:01:54,627
Three: because it has a cause, it can end.

22
00:01:57,667 --> 00:02:00,749
And four: there is a path that ends it.

23
00:02:00,749 --> 00:02:06,067
Notice the shape of that argument — it is diagnostic, like medicine.

24
00:02:06,067 --> 00:02:09,296
Symptom, cause, prognosis, treatment.

25
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:16,238
And the path has eight parts, which is why the wheel has eight spokes.

26
00:02:16,238 --> 00:02:18,173
Right view, right intention.

27
00:02:18,173 --> 00:02:21,243
Right speech, right action, right livelihood.

28
00:02:21,243 --> 00:02:25,146
Right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.

29
00:02:27,933 --> 00:02:31,969
They group into three: wisdom, ethical conduct, and mental discipline.

30
00:02:31,969 --> 00:02:34,356
The whole thing is called the Middle Way,

31
00:02:34,356 --> 00:02:40,168
because it runs between indulgence and self-mortification — the two things he had already tried.

32
00:02:42,167 --> 00:02:43,711
Two things made it spread.

33
00:02:43,711 --> 00:02:50,418
He taught in Prakrit, the language people actually spoke, rather than in Sanskrit, which most could not follow.

34
00:02:54,800 --> 00:03:01,686
And he founded the sangha, an order open to people regardless of birth, which gave the teaching an institution to carry it.

35
00:03:01,686 --> 00:03:08,982
The order admitted women as nuns, though under additional rules, and the texts record debate about that decision at the time.

36
00:03:14,733 --> 00:03:16,715
Even the dates are argued.

37
00:03:16,715 --> 00:03:25,089
The traditional reckoning puts his life at about 563 to 483 BCE, and that is what most Indian textbooks give.

38
00:03:27,333 --> 00:03:30,957
A good deal of recent scholarship argues for a later date,

39
00:03:30,957 --> 00:03:36,792
with his death closer to 400 BCE. Textbooks and examiners generally use the traditional dates,

40
00:03:36,792 --> 00:03:40,347
so learn those — but know that the question is open.

41
00:03:44,833 --> 00:03:48,048
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

42
00:03:48,048 --> 00:03:49,067
Four truths.

43
00:03:49,067 --> 00:03:50,087
Eight steps.

44
00:03:50,087 --> 00:03:51,641
The middle way.

45
00:03:56,267 --> 00:03:57,051
Dig Quiz.

46
00:03:57,051 --> 00:03:59,091
Pause and name the place.

47
00:03:59,091 --> 00:04:03,484
First: where he attained enlightenment, beneath a tree.

48
00:04:03,484 --> 00:04:04,647
Bodh Gaya.

49
00:04:06,667 --> 00:04:11,362
Second: where he gave his first sermon, near Varanasi.

50
00:04:11,362 --> 00:04:12,130
Sarnath.

51
00:04:12,130 --> 00:04:14,008
Third: where he died.

52
00:04:14,008 --> 00:04:15,332
Kushinagar.

53
00:04:20,367 --> 00:04:25,811
In Mains, a common question asks why Buddhism spread so widely in this period.

54
00:04:27,733 --> 00:04:34,940
Use the language, the open order, the support of merchants and kings, and the fit with a society where trade and towns were growing.

55
00:04:38,667 --> 00:04:45,558
A tree above an empty seat takes its place in the gallery: the oldest way Indian art had of showing a person without showing him.

56
00:04:49,167 --> 00:04:53,024
Next gallery: what happened to the teaching after the teacher.

57
00:04:53,024 --> 00:04:56,815
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

